course: french
lesson: 2
title: Funny French sounds: nasal vowels, the French r and u
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: French has some sounds English does not have, and learning them is like learning new moves in a game: strange at first, then easy.
explain: Nasal vowels send the air up through your nose: hold your nose lightly, say bon, and if you feel a buzz, you have it; the n itself is not really said.
explain: The French r is made at the back of the throat, like a soft gargle, not with the tip of the tongue. A handy trick to practise it: say "k", then slide back into a soft, breathy scrape.
explain: The French u has no English twin: say "ee", freeze your tongue, then push your lips into a small circle, and you are saying tu.
explain: Some letter pairs have fixed sounds: ch says "sh", j says the "zh" in "measure", qu says "k" and th says "t", so le chat (the cat) is "luh SHA".
fact: bon means good; on is a nasal vowel, so the air goes through the nose and the n is not said as in English "bonnet".
fact: an, en, am and em usually make one nasal sound, "ahn": la France, maman (mum), un enfant (a child).
fact: ain, in and ein make another nasal sound, a bit like "an" in "cat" sent up the nose: le pain (bread), le vin (wine), le lapin (rabbit), la main (hand).
fact: In French, pain means bread, not hurt.
fact: The French r is made at the back of the mouth, like a soft gargle: rouge (red) sounds like "ROOZH".
fact: tu means you (to a friend) and uses the French u; tout means all and sounds like English "too".
fact: ille usually says "ee-y": la fille (the girl or daughter) is "la FEE-y" and la famille is "la fa-MEE-y"; ville (town) and mille (thousand) are odd ones that keep the l sound: "VEEL", "MEEL".
fact: ch in French says "sh": le chat (cat) is "luh SHA", le chocolat is "luh sho-ko-LA" and le chien (dog) is "luh SHYAN".
fact: The French j is soft, like the middle of "measure": je (I) is "zhuh", jaune (yellow) is "ZHOHN".
fact: qu almost always says "k" in French: quatre (four) is "KATR", qui (who) is "KEE".
fact: French has no English th sound; th is just t: le thé (tea) is "luh TAY" and le théâtre is "luh tay-ATR".
fact: deux (2) sounds like "duh" with round lips, while douze (12) sounds like "DOOZ".
fact: Slang here: "tamping" (Wales) means very cross; "bare" (London) means lots of; "yeah nah" (Australia) means no; "yinz" (Pittsburgh) means you all; "howay" (North East England) means come on; "proper job" (West Country) means great; "chur" (New Zealand) means thanks or cool; "eh up" (Yorkshire) is a way of saying hey.
exercise: How does a French person say bon (good)? | Through the nose, with no real n at the end | Like the English word "bone" | With a full English n, as in "bonnet" | Like the town "Bonn", ending on a clear n
exercise: Which words share the nasal sound "ahn"? | France, maman and enfant | France, bonne and Anne said the English way | pain, main and vin | tu, rue and une
exercise: Ethan says pain at dinner with his French host family. What did he really say? | bread | ouch, I'm hurting | a window pane | paint
exercise: How do you say le pain? | "luh PAN", with a nasal sound | "luh PAYN", like English pain | "luh PAY-in", in two parts | "luh PAN-uh", with an extra sound
exercise: Where is the French r made? | At the back of the mouth, like a soft gargle | With the tip of the tongue rolled fast | With the lips pressed together | With the tongue between the teeth
exercise: Charlie says tu and tout both as "too". Which one is wrong? | tu, which needs the French u | tout, which needs the French u | neither, they are the same sound | both, they should be said "tow"
exercise: What does tu mean? | you, to a friend | all of it, everything | the number two | too much
exercise: How does la fille (the girl) end? | With a little "y" sound: "FEE-y" | With a clear "l": "FILL" | With "fill-uh", like a filling | With a "ya" sound: "fee-YA"
exercise: Which word is an odd one that keeps its l sound? | ville (town) | fille (girl) | famille (family) | gentille (kind, for a girl)
exercise: What does le chat mean, and how does it sound? | the cat, "luh SHA" | the talk, "luh CHAT" | the hat, "luh HAT" | the cat, "luh CHAT"
exercise: How does je (I) start? | With the "zh" sound in the middle of "measure" | With a hard "j" sound, as in the English word "jam" | With a "y" sound, as in "yes" | With a "dj" sound, as in "jeans"
exercise: How does quatre (four) sound? | "KATR" | "KWAT-ra", with a w | "KWOR-ter", like a quarter | "KWAT-ray"
exercise: How do you say le thé (tea)? | "luh TAY" | "luh THAY", tongue between the teeth | "luh TEE", rhyming with "bee" | "luh THEE"
exercise: Kai hears deux and douze. What is the difference? | deux is 2 and sounds "duh"; douze is 12 and sounds "DOOZ" | They mean the same and sound the same | deux is 12 and douze is 2 | deux is said "DOOZ" and douze is "duh"
exercise: Which way of practising the r is helpful? | Say "k", then slide into a soft breathy scrape | Roll your tongue fast like a Spanish r | Say "w" instead, the French won't notice | Never practise it, it comes on its own
test: Harry in Swansea moans, "That's tamping, I can't say bon." ("Tamping" means fuming.) What tip would help? | Hold your nose lightly and feel the buzz | Say the n twice as loud as normal | Add an e at the end: "bonnay" | Say it in English, the French won't mind
test: Sophie argues, "bare people say Fraance like a dance move." ("Bare" means lots of.) How should France sound in French? | "FRAHNSS", with "ahn" through the nose | "FRANCE", with a flat a and a clear n | "FRAN-see", with an extra sound | "FROHNS", rhyming with "bones"
test: Ruby in Dublin says the French r is "like gargling, sure I'll choke." Is she wrong? | Not really: it is a soft scrape at the back, like a gentle gargle | Yes, the French r is rolled at the front, like in Spanish | Yes, French r is always silent, so you skip it | Not really, but it is done with the lips
test: A cartoon cat says "Je m'appelle Jam" with a hard "j", as in "jam". What is wrong? | French j is soft, like the "s" in "measure" | French j is always said "y", as in "yes" | Nothing, j is said the same in both languages | French j is silent at the start of words
test: Leo's mate in Toronto goes, "Bud, quatre is like a quarter." What is the truth? | qu says just "k", so it is "KATR" | Yes, it is "KWOR-ter", like 25 cents | qu says "kw", so it is "KWAT-ra" | It is "CHAT-ra", with a soft ch
test: Freya in Bristol says, "Proper job, but where's the th in thé?" ("Proper job" means great.) What should she know? | French th is just a plain t sound | The th is said like "this" in English | The th is said like "thing" in English | French people forgot to say it long ago
test: Aiden in Philly says chat is "chat, like texting, yinz know." ("Yinz" means you all.) What does the French word mean? | a cat, said "SHA" | a talk, said "CHAT" | a hat, said "SHAT" | a chick, said "SHATT"
test: Jack in Brisbane says "fill" for fille. "Yeah nah, it's 'fee'," says his mum. ("Yeah nah" means no.) Who is closer? | His mum: fille is "FEE-y", with a little y | Jack: fille rhymes with "Bill" | Both: either way is fine | Neither: fille is "fee-LAY"
test: Mia goes, "Howay, je is like Jeremy, innit." ("Howay" means come on.) What sound should she use? | "zhuh", like the middle of "treasure" | "jee", the same sound as at the start of "Jeremy" | "yuh", as in "yes" | "chuh", as in "church"
test: What would happen if you said le pain like the English word in a French bakery? | You would sound wrong, and pain still means bread in French | They would think you were hurting and fetch you a plaster at once | They would give you a window pane | Nothing at all, it sounds exactly the same
test: ★★★ Lily writes down how she heard four words: "SHA", "zhuh", "KATR", "TAY". Which list of spellings matches them in order? | chat, je, quatre, thé | sac, je, quatre, thé | chat, jaune, quatre, thé | chat, je, quatorze, thé
test: ★★★ In which group does every word have a nasal vowel? | bon, maman, pain | bon, tu, maman | pain, rouge, vin | chat, main, France
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pronunciation of the French word bon? | The vowel is nasalised and the terminal n is not articulated as in English | The terminal n is doubled and pronounced with considerable emphasis | The vowel is pronounced as in the English word "bone" | The word is pronounced identically to the German city of Bonn
exam: Identify the meaning, in the French language, of the noun pain. | Bread | Physical suffering | A sheet of glass | A substance for painting
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the French consonant r. | It is articulated at the rear of the mouth | It is articulated by rolling the tip of the tongue | It is invariably omitted in speech | It is articulated between the lips
exam: It is given that the words deux and douze are frequently confused. Ascertain their respective numerical values. | Two and twelve respectively | Twelve and two respectively | Two and twenty respectively | Both denote the number two
